Yeah.

46
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:01,040
You mentioned the feedback loop is different.

47
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:01,520
Yes.

48
00:04:01,742 --> 00:04:02,640
Is that kind of big?

49
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:06,640
That's the big difference between those two worlds and why one is moving faster.

50
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:08,400
I think the adoption and the feedback.

51
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:13,440
One is it reliable enough for you to just go and say, okay, let me try what the AI is saying.

52
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:17,040
Or if you run the model three times, does it give you the same output?

53
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:23,760
Or you're kind of dealing with a non deterministic system and you have to see okay which of these three answers are correct.

54
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:32,100
So I think if we can make AI more repeatable, more reliable, I think with these feedback loops will become much faster.

55
00:04:33,614 --> 00:04:33,856
Okay.

56
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:35,296
So we're on that journey.

57
00:04:35,376 --> 00:04:37,776
It's not like it's destiny that the slower feedback loop.

58
00:04:37,856 --> 00:04:38,736
We could get to that place.

59
00:04:39,296 --> 00:04:39,616
Yeah.

60
00:04:40,046 --> 00:04:42,176
On the coding side, since you're doing that too, right?

61
00:04:42,256 --> 00:04:43,428
And your team is doing that.

62
00:04:44,174 --> 00:04:45,092
Is it just working?

63
00:04:45,536 --> 00:04:47,936
It is working, but it can quickly go out of control.

64
00:04:48,016 --> 00:04:58,976
If you leave it all to AI and do like wipe coding, then it will you probably lose control and you can't ensure that whatever is written inside those files that is exactly how it needs to be.

65
00:04:59,136 --> 00:05:03,456
But I think spec-driven development is something that we really advocate.

66
00:05:03,536 --> 00:05:11,936
And basically, you design each module, you understand what is happening, and then code generation part is what you amplify with the help of these coding agents.

67
00:05:12,176 --> 00:05:20,496
So the designing, the architecture is still something experts would do, and then you just speed up the whole implementation through the coding process.

68
00:05:20,576 --> 00:05:21,860
So the through the co-pilots.

69
00:05:22,830 --> 00:05:23,136
Okay.

70
00:05:23,566 --> 00:05:24,536
So spec driven

71
00:05:24,536 --> 00:05:25,376
spec driven development.

72
00:05:26,816 --> 00:05:34,656
Spec-driven development, which to me sounds a lot like going back to the future of what that's like that otherwise known as great engineering.

73
00:05:35,790 --> 00:05:36,016
Yeah.

74
00:05:36,176 --> 00:05:36,496
Okay.

75
00:05:36,846 --> 00:05:39,216
So there's a discipline there that doesn't go away.

76
00:05:39,456 --> 00:05:39,776
Absolutely.

77
00:05:40,016 --> 00:05:55,456
Well then, so if you look at the the bigger prize from an economic perspective, but also amongst a lot of our listeners who are running enterprises and aren't just responsible for code generation, but also for enterprise transformation.

78
00:05:55,630 --> 00:05:55,936
Yes.

79
00:05:56,336 --> 00:06:03,216
if the feedback loop is the problem, what are some of the things you're doing to grapple with that problem?

80
00:06:03,376 --> 00:06:05,604
Is that the number one thing, or are there other challenges?

81
00:06:06,336 --> 00:06:20,576
It is in my mind the number one thing because if you have feedback, then of course you can better train your models, you have better examples to show, you can fine-tune your models why the users are not taking up your recommendation why they are deviating.

82
00:06:20,686 --> 00:06:22,736
So the whole thing just becomes faster.

83
00:06:22,896 --> 00:06:25,988
But I think if you open up that box, okay, why it is not happening.

84
00:06:26,256 --> 00:06:28,736
So I just want to like base it on key things.

85
00:06:28,896 --> 00:06:30,692
One is the reliability of the system.

86
00:06:31,214 --> 00:06:37,456
If the systems are more reliable, more repeatable, then of course there is higher confidence, along with, of course, explainable.

87
00:06:37,536 --> 00:06:38,896
So there is a higher confidence.

88
00:06:39,182 --> 00:06:50,656
And the second is as some of these models become more expensive, how do you deal with the cost side of things, still keeping it viable for the business to make money out of these implementations?

89
00:06:50,736 --> 00:06:54,096
And of course, there are talks about activity gains and so on.

90
00:06:55,022 --> 00:06:58,816
But the cost efficiency and the reliability is the two sides.

91
00:06:59,056 --> 00:06:59,456
And

92
00:06:59,456 --> 00:07:02,976
what happens is often these two things are treated independently.

93
00:07:03,296 --> 00:07:07,140
You look at cost optimization as a separate topic and reliability as a separate topic.

94
00:07:07,376 --> 00:07:16,576
But the realization for me has been that okay, there are key fundamental things that if we follow them well, then both cost and reliability go hand in hand.

95
00:07:16,656 --> 00:07:20,736
It's not that you have to pay a lot more for a better reliable system.

96
00:07:20,896 --> 00:07:30,084
So as these capabilities are getting enhanced, the genetic capabilities, there are also new patterns, new ways of doing things that are emerging, which leads to this kind of a convergence.

97
00:07:32,336 --> 00:07:32,896
Make that real.

98
00:07:32,976 --> 00:07:34,416
So how does that actually manifest?

99
00:07:34,736 --> 00:07:36,656
You say that those things go hand in hand.

100
00:07:36,896 --> 00:07:37,216
Absolutely.

101
00:07:37,376 --> 00:07:39,588
We have heard about LLMs, we've all heard about tools.

102
00:07:39,950 --> 00:07:42,816
So the large language model is basically the brain of the whole system.

103
00:07:43,136 --> 00:07:46,736
This is what which thinks, it is something that can reason and so on.

104
00:07:46,896 --> 00:07:49,604
Whereas tools is something which is very specific.

105
00:07:49,870 --> 00:08:00,132
Uh, tool, for example, go a fetch some information for you, or they can do like, okay, how do I read this PDF or how do I read this proprietary you know, file?

106
00:08:00,686 --> 00:08:04,896
You did you develop a tool and then the tool does exactly in a deterministic manner.

107
00:08:05,056 --> 00:08:09,536
It's not, it's not like intelligent, but it is it does it's like a function.

108
00:08:09,616 --> 00:08:12,816
You call the function and it does the job that is required.

109
00:08:13,006 --> 00:08:15,616
So this is the construct we all are very familiar with.

110
00:08:15,776 --> 00:08:17,376
And then came the MCP servers.

111
00:08:17,456 --> 00:08:21,124
Now, MCP servers was okay, let's make these tools reusable.

112
00:08:21,486 --> 00:08:29,536
If I build something, you should be able to use that tool, and the whole org should be able to access that tool, let's say, to an MCP server or an external tool.

113
00:08:29,776 --> 00:08:33,668
So what we did was instead of we kind of made these tools reusable.

114
00:08:34,336 --> 00:08:44,976
But if you look at the problem that got generated on the on the token consumption side, each of these tools they live as JSON schemas in the code.

115
00:08:45,216 --> 00:08:58,176
And every time there is an LLM needs to decide, it has to kind of look at the entire definition of the tool, the arguments, and it just adds on to the number of tokens that you have.

116
00:08:58,256 --> 00:09:07,648
So if you have more than 30, 40 tools, your context window is already filled up with that entire load of information about these tools.

117
00:09:07,854 --> 00:09:12,048
From an outside, I was saying, okay, I'm adding more capabilities to my model, right?

118
00:09:12,208 --> 00:09:13,408
The model can do 10 more,

119
00:09:13,408 --> 00:09:13,728
60.

120
00:09:14,048 --> 00:09:14,288
Exactly.

121
00:09:14,528 --> 00:09:18,208
But on the flip side, you are filling up your context window.

122
00:09:18,288 --> 00:09:23,488
So by the time you get to the actual user question, your context window is already, let's say, 50% free.

123
00:09:23,728 --> 00:09:29,488
There's no room to ask the question you want to answer because everything has already been asked just to be able to build the tool connections.

124
00:09:29,728 --> 00:09:34,128
Yeah, at least a good percentage of your context window is already filled up with these tool definitions.

125
00:09:34,288 --> 00:09:36,288
And what happens is this is not just one time.

126
00:09:36,448 --> 00:09:42,208
Every turn that happens, this definition goes to the LLM, just rotating.

127
00:09:42,288 --> 00:09:50,596
So you are using, you are inefficient with your context window and you are spending a lot of tokens just passing back and forth these tool definitions.

128
00:09:52,174 --> 00:09:53,128
So that's like

129
00:09:53,128 --> 00:09:53,568
weird.

130
00:09:53,888 --> 00:09:56,928
Most users have no clue that this is going on in the background.

131
00:09:57,248 --> 00:10:06,128
They're thinking, at least I and I'm as a business leader in former life and currently, my national conclusion is more tools access good.

132
00:10:06,382 --> 00:10:07,168
Yeah, exactly.

133
00:10:07,408 --> 00:10:09,328
Because as you said, they have a big function.

134
00:10:09,488 --> 00:10:20,528
The narrative around, if I may, around agents that I see a lot of times is we use this word orchestration, which is sounds super cool and I gather that it's real in one sense.

135
00:10:20,782 --> 00:10:30,288
People are figuring out how to orchestrate, but the more you're orchestrating with access to a bunch of tools, the reality is when you peel the onion, you're suddenly banging into this other constraint.

136
00:10:30,528 --> 00:10:31,168
Yes, exactly.

137
00:10:31,408 --> 00:10:32,996
Which may be not visible.

138
00:10:33,262 --> 00:10:38,608
So orchestration, yes, but then at what cost and with effectiveness.

139
00:10:38,848 --> 00:10:39,088
Yes.

140
00:10:39,168 --> 00:10:41,248
And also the model starts to hallucinate.

141
00:10:41,328 --> 00:10:46,448
The more tools you have, the harder it becomes for the model to choose the right tool to get the job done.

142
00:10:46,702 --> 00:10:51,108
So there is also a selection overhead that is added in this process.

143
00:10:51,918 --> 00:10:54,208
So that's the third way of the triangle from a challenge perspective.

144
00:10:54,368 --> 00:10:54,448
Yes.

145
00:10:54,688 --> 00:10:58,208
But you're working on this for real right now in certain client context.

146
00:10:58,688 --> 00:10:58,928
Exactly.

147
00:10:59,246 --> 00:11:01,248
So this is where the skills come in.

148
00:11:01,408 --> 00:11:03,568
You can think of skills like playbooks, right?

149
00:11:03,728 --> 00:11:09,488
If you have to, let's say, assess a supplier or you have to close your books at the end of the month.

150
00:11:09,568 --> 00:11:11,808
There is a certain way in which you do it.

151
00:11:11,968 --> 00:11:19,088
And you want that to be organization specific, you want it repeatable, and you want this to be done exactly this way.

152
00:11:19,278 --> 00:11:21,168
So this is where the skills come in.

153
00:11:21,328 --> 00:11:30,368
It's not code, but it's like MD file, markdown file, like a YAML file, which allows the LLM to see how things need to be done.

154
00:11:30,528 --> 00:11:38,276
So instead of adding layers of prompts, we just give a skill to the model, and the model can just get more efficient with these playbooks.

155
00:11:39,278 --> 00:11:40,288
Okay, gotcha.

156
00:11:40,928 --> 00:11:47,408
So that's that's a workaround that allows you to have the same functionality, or is there a trade-off there versus just calling the tool?

157
00:11:47,662 --> 00:11:52,516
I won't say it's a trade-off because skills can in turn call the tools to get something done.

158
00:11:52,688 --> 00:11:53,008
Oh, okay.

159
00:11:53,168 --> 00:11:58,048
But but you're wrapping up how it needs to be done in a specific instruction set.

160
00:11:58,606 --> 00:11:59,488
So that's the skill.

161
00:11:59,648 --> 00:12:04,676
And the good part is the whole skill doesn't sit into your context window.

162
00:12:04,848 --> 00:12:08,368
Only the name and the description, which is like less than 80 tokens.

163
00:12:08,608 --> 00:12:10,208
Ah, so compressed in essence.

164
00:12:10,448 --> 00:12:10,928
Very compressed.

165
00:12:11,008 --> 00:12:14,128
So only the name and the description that sits in your context window.

166
00:12:14,288 --> 00:12:18,468
And the model, when it realizes, okay, now I have to do a monthly closing.

167
00:12:18,830 --> 00:12:20,528
Let me see if I have a skill for that.

168
00:12:20,688 --> 00:12:24,452
If I find a skill, yes, now let me load the skill to get the job done.

169
00:12:25,262 --> 00:12:28,228
But is this kind of solve the problem, or is there still something left?

170
00:12:28,688 --> 00:12:43,888
So I think this is solved for the problem that you have not loaded everything into your context window, but you are making things repeatable, but at the same time, you are kind of allowing the LLM to choose the right skill at the lower context overhead to do that.

171
00:12:44,238 --> 00:12:44,448
Okay.

172
00:12:44,686 --> 00:12:49,728
So as a lay person, I use Claude skills for just doing Excel modeling, right?

173
00:12:49,888 --> 00:12:51,488
Because it has an Excel skill.

174
00:12:51,694 --> 00:12:51,968
Yes.

175
00:12:52,128 --> 00:12:54,608
And I'm not advertising for Claude because I know there's other.

176
00:12:55,150 --> 00:12:59,568
Yeah, there are pretty much every LLM has a different name for the skill now.

177
00:12:59,648 --> 00:13:03,396
So you can do it in OpenAI, you can do it in other LLM that is out there.

178
00:13:04,430 --> 00:13:07,312
But at the same time, it's still sharing the same context window.

179
00:13:07,472 --> 00:13:10,592
So you're not like invented a new context window.

180
00:13:10,672 --> 00:13:12,512
It's it's still part of the same context window.

181
00:13:12,752 --> 00:13:19,044
It's just that you have reduced like 50 tools to let's say 10 skills, and then you are calling the skills.

182
00:13:19,232 --> 00:13:26,592
But let's say if you have to use the whole context window in a very fresh way, you'd want a new instance of a context window.

183
00:13:26,752 --> 00:13:28,676
So that's where these sub-agents come in.

184
00:13:28,942 --> 00:13:34,112
You can think of them as departments in your organizations or employees with specializations.

185
00:13:34,352 --> 00:13:41,632
They have been prompted and they have been instructed in a certain way to act as a specialist in that specific area, right?

186
00:13:41,998 --> 00:13:46,752
And the good part is when you spin up a sub-agent, it has its whole new context window.

187
00:13:46,832 --> 00:13:49,472
So you're starting from scratch in that context window.

188
00:13:49,712 --> 00:13:51,072
These are little nuances.

189
00:13:51,152 --> 00:13:59,392
I know I'm getting technical, but this is where things become really interesting that, okay, which pattern do you choose for what kind of problems?

190
00:13:59,632 --> 00:14:02,340
Do you use a tool versus a skill versus a sub-agent?

191
00:14:02,512 --> 00:14:09,312
So this is a selection that becomes very important because I know sometimes you need to solve for speed, sometimes you need to solve for parallelism.

192
00:14:09,486 --> 00:14:17,060
In one of the case for example, there were different tickets that would come in, and each ticket would need to be handled in a parallel way.

193
00:14:17,152 --> 00:14:28,580
And each of these skills, the subagents can be spinned up as a separate entity for each ticket, and you can do that kind of a processing in a subagent, which is completely independent of everything else.

194
00:14:28,974 --> 00:14:35,396
You can spin 50 of them, get the results done, and then they feed back to the main agent, and then the workflow continues.

195
00:14:35,792 --> 00:14:40,592
So that would be one way to also bring parallelism along with context window efficiency.

196
00:14:42,318 --> 00:14:44,832
It is to your point about knowing which problem you're solving.

197
00:14:44,992 --> 00:14:58,052
That's another crucial gap that I see, just observing folks who are practically inclined but don't necessarily have the experience to sort of say, wait, what yeah, what's the business problem and how do I solve it?

198
00:14:58,350 --> 00:15:00,352
Are you suggesting kind of a method here?

199
00:15:00,512 --> 00:15:03,332
Like if you want parallelism, you go down path A.

200
00:15:03,758 --> 00:15:06,752
If you want speed, it's path B, or like how should somebody think about that?

201
00:15:06,992 --> 00:15:11,712
If it is a very deterministic process, needs to be done exactly in the same way.

202
00:15:11,872 --> 00:15:15,712
There is no intelligence required, or you have to touch the outside world.

203
00:15:15,792 --> 00:15:21,072
I say, okay, I go and fetch something from my partner from the web or from a database.

204
00:15:21,152 --> 00:15:22,272
So that's a tool, clearly.

205
00:15:22,352 --> 00:15:22,948
It's a tool.

206
00:15:23,232 --> 00:15:23,552
Okay.

207
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:24,032
Right.

208
00:15:24,192 --> 00:15:34,592
And the moment you say, okay, this is repeatable, this is non-obvious, needs a little bit of org-specific behavior, that's a skill for sure.

209
